{"id":3908,"date":"2021-10-25T13:56:30","date_gmt":"2021-10-25T20:56:30","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=3908"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:27:51","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:27:51","slug":"2-2-flight-directions-senior-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/2-2-flight-directions-senior-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"2.2: Flight Directions"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355ca953\" name=\"6a221355ca953\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761251260724{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355caacb\" name=\"6a221355caacb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Movements<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Air vehicles move a little differently than land vehicles. While land vehicles can move forward, backward, and sometimes side-to-side, air vehicles can do all of that and move up and down. To keep track of how a CoDrone EDU moves in a three-dimensional space, you can use the terms below:<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<div>\n<div><strong>Roll<\/strong>\u00a0controls the CoDrone EDU\u2019s horizontal, or side to side, movement. Positive roll will make the CoDrone EDU move to the right, and negative roll will make the drone move to the left.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6145&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div><strong>Pitch<\/strong> is the CoDrone EDU\u2019s forward and backward tilt. Positive pitch will make the CoDrone EDU tilt and move forward, and negative pitch will make the CoDrone EDU tilt and move backwards.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6144&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<b>Yaw <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">is the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s left and right rotation. Positive yaw will make the CoDrone EDU turn to the left, and negative yaw will make the CoDrone EDU turn to the right.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6146&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<b>Throttle <\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">controls the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s vertical, or up and down, movement. Positive throttle will make the CoDrone EDU fly higher, and negative throttle will make the CoDrone EDU fly lower.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;6143&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Be careful with yaw and roll! Yaw will make your CoDrone EDU turn left or right, while roll will move your CoDrone EDU left or right.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355cac2f\" name=\"6a221355cac2f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Blind Drone<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Have a friend sit down, blindfold them (or just have them keep their eyes closed), and guide them through an obstacle course to sit down in a different chair using only flight movement words. Make sure that anything dangerous is out of their way first!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive throttle: stand up\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Negative throttle: sit down\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive pitch: one step forward<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Negative pitch: one step backward<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive yaw: turn left\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Negative yaw: turn right<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Positive roll: move one step to the right<\/span><\/li>\n<li><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Negative roll: move one step to the left<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For example, if you wanted to tell your friend to move forward, you could say, &#8220;Positive pitch 2 steps.&#8221; That would let your friend know that you want them to move forward by 2 steps. For turning, say &#8220;Positive yaw 45&#8221; and your friend should turn to the left 45 degrees. Once your friend successfully completes the obstacle course, switch roles so they can give you directions!\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355cacf6\" name=\"6a221355cacf6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Flight Variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can also use the blocks in the aqua &#8220;Flight Variables&#8221; menu to move your drone. These blocks work exactly the same way as the blue<strong> move<\/strong> block, but their syntax more closely resembles Python. You can stack blocks to make your CoDrone EDU go in one or more directions at once (like diagonally to the right), and then use the aqua <strong>move()<\/strong> block afterward.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;5586&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try the code below to see what happens!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7610&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><strong>Warning<\/strong>:<em> if you want to see distinct directions one after the other, you will need to set the previously flown direction back to 0% before adding on the new flight direction. Otherwise, your drone will keep performing that action throughout the entire program. Try the program below after testing out the program above to see what we are describing.<\/em><\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;5588&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355cade9\" name=\"6a221355cade9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Making Turns<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>In addition to being able to fly up, down, left, right, forward, and backward, your CoDrone EDU can also turn left and right using the <strong>turn_degree()<\/strong> function in the &#8220;Flight Commands&#8221; menu.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10181&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Unlike the Junior function, this block will not turn the specified number of degrees that you set. Instead, it will turn to that <em>degree<\/em> <em>heading<\/em>. Imagine a compass on the floor that resets every time you pair the drone.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10183&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Run this code to illustrate how <em>absolute heading <\/em>works. CoDrone EDU will take off, turn left 90 degrees, then turn left again 30 degrees to reach 120 degrees.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10184&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>If you want to reset your &#8220;compass&#8221; back to 0 degrees, you can use the <strong>reset_sensor()<\/strong> block from the orange &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu. You will learn more about the sensors in a later lesson.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10185&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355cae6d\" name=\"6a221355cae6d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Move Block<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>Now that you have had a little practice with the individual flight commands, you can also combine the movements into one block. This block also makes it easier for you to reset any other variables back to 0. The function&#8217;s parameters\u00a0are roll, pitch, yaw, throttle, and duration, in that order.<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<div>For example, guess the flight pattern of the code below. Then, run the program. <em>Were you right?<\/em><\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10186&#8243; img_size=&#8221;full&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355caed9\" name=\"6a221355caed9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div><strong>Chutes and Ladders<\/strong><\/div>\n<div>Now that you know how to use flight variables to control the CoDrone EDU, try programming your CoDrone EDU to fly like it is climbing a ladder and then going down a spiral slide!<\/div>\n<div>Here are some hints:<\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>Try to make your CoDrone EDU climb the ladder like a set of stairs.<\/li>\n<li>To make your CoDrone EDU \u2018spiral\u2019 down the slide, trying turning while flying down at the same time!<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221355caf2e\" name=\"6a221355caf2e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>In this lesson, you learned about the concept of flight variables. Roll, pitch, yaw, and throttle are all used to control the CoDrone EDU\u2019s directional movement in the air. These flight variables are also seen in other aviation uses, such as helicopters and planes!<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 9 We've Moved! [vc_column_